swer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "False"] ["text" "True"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-341129" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "True"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-341129" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-341129" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "False"] ["text" "True"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-341129" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "True"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-342406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ -2, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-342406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/7cfd6da222fb36d06be1153ac0e398e567f8b9fb8cd1cd9e21f1bb35ed131f8d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+1)=-2+1=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2)=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/a59fad69c9790a2b899c21fd730f779c28bc9b70f44a67851ad5ca92bff21951.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" 