e_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-342406" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "False"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-342657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+2, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-342657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because $\\frac{1}{x}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/8e5eec10427a7061a05c0bd1228ce1146234bf2b09d75bb86ba83b28fbe178d9.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+2)=-2+2=0\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/4afcdf86f777b9c7afc8af31b36cde4b80cfe394fd908db1ccd47ef48dff1617.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-342657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16a78fa02682c8710ae4fea2e334ad4fe71d7da89ab8b6f4e0fa5d230e3f7846"} {"question" "q-342657" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-342657" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "False"] ["text" "True"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-34265