xtension of the function $F(x)=\\frac{3}{2}x$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-343340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {[\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-343340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16a78fa02682c8710ae4fea2e334ad4fe71d7da89ab8b6f4e0fa5d230e3f7846"} {"question" "q-343340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-343340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-343340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0ef6d55925293a63fd3c25a7345dea904c95e009d8872ee551c3b5128c1c58"} {"question" "q-343340" "field" "field-2" 