db538b8a1fef7153"} {"question" "e-21846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/5c1cc57825a318df088ac0bc28ffe75c21579c5fe007fbb52cf0710a1937dc1e.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/f5fb59869184a0c54ddaeea0b434104f3335c652c3f7533cd9dec0dcdcc91876.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/example-21846.json" "file_sha256" "e7d6b85ea864cbbec5182856779260e3d3b1b8f91bf6b636db538b8a1fef7153"} {"question" "e-21845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function\n$F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$\ndefined on $[-5,5).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension of $G(x)=5x$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/178be718fdba262f895193f9c503667f4ba3f709db23717893016d500ba4bc8b.png)\nSince $f$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/6c274adc750119956ce950c5212283e01dfddf37137178bf0b85295a6837f433.png)\nand  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/6c274adc750119956ce950c5212283e01dfddf37137178bf0b85295a6837f433.png)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/9d14fd1a227fc073b09d78684cb8dbff507bf083da988dda2755270a77981455.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/example-21845.json" "file_sha256" "06c14715f65b556d1a212997dd9196a774cbf1bd778ac0ccd42e17ab25a1a4c7"} {"question" "e-21845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous} }$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{107}{6})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/example-21845.json" "file_sha256" "06c14715f65b556d1a212997dd9196a774cbf1bd778ac0ccd42e17ab25a1a4c7"} {"question" "e-21856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\dfrac{3}{4}x^2$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{4}x^3 = \\int_0^x f(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/7ddcc5e5d8580428341aec84ff2982c593054ec7ec363cc11139d87912dab58d.png)\n Since $f$ is \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/91f9e216819e2259c6cc4ce19c39e38f8d75c9a5902019de9e437e0732477943.png)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/1c6cf5957b07d2564607d603276b7fb9cffbe7502f53c6ea77a10b726d95aefd.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/example-21856.json" "file_sha256" "be0572ebb6ef33321d738d662f52bbd9164b0524b6a7875a9bdbfab859662096"} {"question" "e-21856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n \\geq 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{[-4t]}_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3} & ∼-4x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{192(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/example-21856.json" "file_sha256" "be0572ebb6ef33321d738d662f52bbd9164b0524b6a7875a9bdbfab859662096"}] "usage" {"e-21846" [] "q-343380" [] "e-21856" [] "q-341129" [] "e-21845" []} "basis" 2346 "retractions" [[17592186221961 :question/problem "$[MATH: F(x) = {x^{2}, \\mid x ∈ [0, 3); - 3x, \\mid x ∈ [- 3, 0)]$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $[MATH: x ∈ [- 3, 3).]$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x ∈ R$.\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x ∈ (-3, 3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x ∈ (-3, 3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $lim_(x → - 3^{+})F(x) = lim_(x → 3^{-})F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186221974 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of the function $F(x) =-\\frac{3}{2}x^{2}$ defined on $[MATH: [- 2, 2).]$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $4$-periodic extension of\n$G(x) =-\\frac{1}{2}x^{3} = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$.The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n$\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{4})$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186221142 :question/problem "$[MATH: {-2x, \\mid x ∈ [- 2, 0); x^{2}, \\mid x ∈ [0, 2)]$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $[MATH: x \\in [-2,2).]$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\n1. $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2)$.\n2. $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2)$.\n3. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x)$.\n4. $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$."] [17592186221142 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2)$. Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6383/Source/Images/f536c645bf6b1cd0ea1bbc8576eeb69b.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2)$. It consists of two smooth pieces.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4$\nand\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4$.\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x)$.\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6383/Source/Images/b3c8ff64307e627d983401287be632ae.png)"] [17592186221141 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function\n$F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$\ndefined on $[MATH: [-5,5).]$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension of $G(x)=5x$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$.\n\n> The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n> $XXXXX$\n> Since $f$ is $XXXXXXXX$\n> and $XXXXXXXX$\n> on $\\mathbb{R}$, we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n> $XXXXXX$"] [17592186221141 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5$. Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2} \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5)$,\n- continuous on $[MATH: [-5,5),]$ and\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5)$, if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$.\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R}$, so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{5}{2}x^{2} - 3) & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{107}{6}) + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{d}{dx}((250(-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2})\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(250(-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2}) \\cdot (-\\frac{nπ}{5}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(-50(-1)^{n})/(nπ)\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{5}) \\\\ 5x & ∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)50 (-1) n + 1 n π \\cdot sin ⁡ (n π x 5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186221143 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\dfrac{3}{4}x^2$ defined on $[MATH: [-4,4).]$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of\n$G(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{4}x^3 = \\displaystyle \\int_0^x f(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$.\n\n> The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n> $XXXX$\n> Since $f$ is\n> $xxxxxxxxxxx$\n> on $\\mathbb{R}$, we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n> $XXXX$"] [17592186221143 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nThus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2} \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4)$.\nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\int_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}t^{2})dt & ∼\\int_{0}^{x}(-4 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(48(-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{2}π^{2})\\cos (\\frac{nπt}{4}))dt \\\\ [- \\frac{1}{4}t^{3}]_{0}^{x} & ∼[- 4t]_{0}^{x} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(48(-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{2}π^{2}) \\cdot [\\frac{4}{nπ}\\sin (\\frac{nπt}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}x^{3} & ∼ - 4x + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)192 (-1) n + 1 n 3 π 3 \\cdot sin ⁡ (n π x 4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220739 17592186220740 17592186220742 17592186220743 17592186220745 17592186220746 17592186220748 17592186220749 17592186220836 17592186220837 17592186220838 17592186220839] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220738 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220738 :answer-field/id] [17592186220738 :answer-field/key] [17592186220738 :answer-field/type] [17592186220741 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220741 :answer-field/id] [17592186220741 :answer-field/key] [17592186220741 :answer-field/type] [17592186220744 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220744 :answer-field/id] [17592186220744 :answer-field/key] [17592186220744 :answer-field/type] [17592186220747 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220747 :answer-field/id] [17592186220747 :answer-field/key] [17592186220747 :answer-field/type] [17592186220835 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220835 :answer-field/id] [17592186220835 :answer-field/key] [17592186220835 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
